  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 AM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nIncreasing northeast winds on a tightening pressure gradient have\nadvected in an MVFR stratus deck across much of northeast,\ncentral, and east-central Wisconsin early this morning. These \nlow ceilings are expected to gradually lift into a \nstratocumulus/cumulus field by late this morning as diurnal \nheating and dry air advection take hold. Skies should then clear \nfrom north to south this afternoon as strong surface high pressure\nbuilds south out of Ontario. High temperatures today will be \nsomewhat suppressed by the low clouds and onshore flow, ranging \nfrom the mid 70s near Lake Michigan to around 80 degrees across \ncentral Wisconsin.\n\nHigh pressure will center over the western Great Lakes tonight \nthrough Monday night, bringing a period of quiet and pleasant \nweather with near-normal temperatures and comfortable humidity. \nHighs on Monday will reach the upper 70s to mid 80s under mostly \nsunny skies. \n\nThe next weather maker approaches Tuesday as a shortwave trough \nand associated surface cold front track eastward across the Upper \nMidwest. Convective parameters ahead of the boundary appear \nmodest, with MUCAPE values peaking around 500 to 1000 J/kg and \ndeep-layer shear around 25 to 30 knots. This combination suggests \na minimal threat for organized severe weather. However, \nprecipitable water values rising to 120 to 130% of normal \nalongside deep-layer flow aligned parallel to the surface frontal \nboundary could promote localized training of showers and storms, \npresenting a low risk for localized heavy rainfall. Recent model \nruns are a bit drier with the initial showers and thunderstorms, \nparticularly across east-central Wisconsin. In fact, there is a \npossibility most of these areas could remain mostly dry until late\nTuesday night when the main low lifts out of the southern Plains \nand an area of mid level frontogenesis develops across the western\nGreat Lakes.\n\nMost global ensemble guidance rapidly pushes the cold front east\nof the area by Wednesday morning, though lingering light rain\nshowers cannot be ruled out over northeast Wisconsin early\nWednesday. A notable outlier in deterministic guidance (GFS)\nholds the front up and delays its eastern progression,\nmaintaining heavy rainfall potential into Wednesday, but this\nsolution lacks support from ensemble cluster means. High pressure\nbuilds back into the Upper Mississippi Valley behind the front\nfor Wednesday and Thursday, returning dry weather and seasonal\nconditions. The next upper-level shortwave and cold front will\nbring the next opportunity for showers and thunderstorms toward\nnext Friday into Saturday. Temperatures throughout the 7-day\nperiod will remain right around seasonal averages, with daily\nhighs in the mid 70s to mid 80s and overnight lows in the upper\n40s to low 60s.",
